 ],
 "literal": "Pepper-vine-growing on the mountain slope in that place, eating tender sprouts / monkeys together staying, lord of the great mountain land / because he is sweet, than the unpleasantness arising from one's own kin / is it sweeter, / that which is called sweet, the land of the gods?",
 "translation": "In his land, on the mountain slopes where pepper vines grow, monkeys gather together to eat tender sprouts. Since the lord of this great mountain land is so sweet to me, is the world of the gods, which everyone calls sweet, sweeter than even the pain caused by my own kin?",
 "modern": "மிளகுக்கொடி வளரும் மலைச்சரிவிலே, துளிர்களைத் தின்று குரங்குகள் ஒன்றாகக் கூடி வாழும் பெரிய மலைநாட்டை உடையவன் என் தலைவன். அவன் எனக்கு இனியவன் என்பதால், என் சுற்றத்தாரால் ஏற்படும் துன்பத்தை விட, எல்லாரும் இனிது என்று சொல்லும் தேவருலகம் இனிமையானதாக இருக்குமோ?",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend when her family restricts her movements due to their suspicion of her love affair. She compares the pain caused by her relatives to the legendary sweetness of the world of the gods. She declares that because her lover is so sweet, even the suffering she endures for his sake is sweeter than heaven itself. This is a Kuriñci landscape poem, where the image of monkeys peacefully gathering and eating tender shoots on the pepper-growing slopes of the hero's mountain signifies the natural harmony and richness of his land, reflecting his protective nature. Key words include 'பெருங்கனாடன்' (lord of the great mountain land), 'இனியன்' (sweet one), 'இன்னாமை' (unpleasantness/pain), and 'புத்தேள் நாடு' (land of the gods).",
